WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.910
Mad Men is one of the greatest shows in television
history, but it's ending prompted quite a

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:05.660
bit of debate.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.940
All these years later, we’re still discussing
what exactly happened in that last episode,

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.320
but now, we think we finally understand the
ending of Mad Men.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.620
In "Lost Horizon," the twelfth episode of
Mad Men's seventh and final season, Don Draper

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.120
decides to take a road trip.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.750
He does so after attending a pitch meeting
at McCann Erickson, where he feels lost, irrelevant,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:25.600
and disinterested.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.020
Don exits the meeting shortly after it begins,
gets in his car, and just starts driving,

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:31.980
and he doesn't tell anyone where he's going.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.320
At the start of "Person to Person," the fourteenth
episode of the seventh season and the final

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:40.519
episode of the show, we learn that Don has
made it all the way to Utah, and he's racing

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:41.500
across the desert.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.920
It's miles away, both literally and figuratively,
from where we're used to seeing him.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.600
Embracing his inner grease monkey, Don opts
for a "Canadian tuxedo" in lieu of one of

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.800
his signature business suits.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.840
Draper no longer feels like he belongs in
New York's advertising world, and he's on

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.060
a mission to discover where he truly belongs.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.600
Soon enough, he discovers his destiny isn't
to break the land speed record in the Utah

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.100
desert.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:08.080
Pete Campbell only shows up in two short scenes
in the finale, but they're both essential

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.400
to his overall character arc.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:14.320
Throughout most of Mad Men's run, Pete was
unbearably smug, selfish, and insincere.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.799
"A man like you I'd follow into combat blindfolded,
and I wouldn't be the first.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:19.800
Am I right, buddy?"

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.420
"Let's take it a little slower."

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.840
But in Mad Men's final season, Pete begins
to change for the better.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.120
In the penultimate episode of Mad Men, he
manages to patch things up with his ex-wife

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:30.620
Trudy.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.960
He also takes a new job with Learjet that
requires moving to Kansas, so he'll be starting

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.280
a whole new life with his family.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.360
In the scene where Pete says goodbye to Peggy,
he's all smiles and clearly has no ulterior

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:41.860
motive.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.060
"Keep it up, you'll be a creative director
by 1980."

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.040
"God that sounds like a long time."

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.500
It's a complete 180 from his sleazy first
encounters with Peggy in the series' premiere.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.700
"Well, you're in the city now, wouldn't be
a sin for us to see your legs, and you pull

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.560
your waist in a little bit, you might look
like a woman."

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.540
Moral of the story?

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.440
People can change, and so can weasels.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.960
Don's fun in the sun is quickly squashed by
some terrible news.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.100
He calls his daughter Sally to excitedly tell
her about his adventures, but she's not interested.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:11.100
"Uh-huh."

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:17.820
"Six hundred and twenty-two miles an hour,
a car that looked like a jet…Sally?"

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.620
Eventually, Sally tells her dad that Betty,
her mother and Don's ex-wife, has been diagnosed

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:23.880
with terminal lung cancer.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:27.910
Don immediately says he'll return to New York
so Sally and her two younger brothers can

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.780
move in with him, but Sally discourages him
from doing so.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:33.700
Undeterred, Don calls Betty and tells her
the same thing:

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:35.480
"I don't want you to worry about them.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:37.200
They're going to come live with me.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.560
You don't even have to ask."

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.060
"I wasn't going to."

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:43.380
Betty is seeing things more clearly as she
approaches death:

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.580
"What they really need is a woman in their
life.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:48.920
A regular family.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.420
Living with my brother and Judy is in their
best interests."

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:56.400
Betty reminds Don that he's never really been
a good father to his children.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.960
It finally hits him that he's not the family
man he always thought himself to be.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.780
This revelation hits him hard.

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:06.800
Despite the fact that Betty is dying and Don
is effectively out of their lives, we still

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.940
get the feeling that the Draper kids, Sally,
Gene, and Bobby, are gonna be alright.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.320
Sally seems intent on not turning into her
parents, and her predicament forces her to

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:16.340
grow up fast.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:20.280
After speaking with her dad on the phone,
the eldest Draper child surprisingly returns

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:24.840
home from boarding school, canceling a trip
abroad to do so, to help raise her two younger

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:25.580
brothers.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.540
After telling Gene to leave the room, she
has a brief discussion with Bobby about their

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:30.420
mother's health.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.760
Although Bobby hasn't been briefed on Betty's
illness, he knows she's dying, and he's been

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.820
trying to pick up the slack around the house.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.791
He just failed at cooking dinner as Sally
comes home, so she decides to show him a few

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:41.720
tricks of the trade.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:46.260
The last time we see Sally, she's washing
dishes while Betty smokes at the kitchen table.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:50.020
It's obvious that Sally is determined to give
her brothers a proper upbringing.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:54.380
Despite the best efforts of Betty and Don,
she's obviously got a good head on her shoulders.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.100
Sometimes people become fantastic individuals
despite their parenting, rather than because

00:03:59.100 --> 00:03:59.600
of it.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.820
Roger Sterling doesn't have a lot to do in
the Mad Men series finale.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.200
His biggest contribution in the seventh season,
and his final grand act, is orchestrating

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.060
the sale of Sterling Cooper &amp; Partners to
McCann Erickson.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:16.080
An older man with no roots at his new company,
Roger wasn't particularly valued at McCann,

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:16.740
and he knew it.

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:20.640
He wasn't the future of the company, and in
his mind, he didn't have much of a future

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.140
at all.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:25.520
In the finale, he claims to be entering the
final chapter of his life, and surprisingly,

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.060
he seems totally okay with that.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.180
Roger's final chapter involves him learning
to love again, and getting his finances in

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:31.680
order.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:37.100
At one point, he visits his old flame, Joan
Holloway Harris, but not to rekindle a romance.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.970
Instead, he tells her that when he dies, he'll
be leaving a substantial portion of his fortune

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.160
to Kevin, the secret son they share together.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:47.880
Joan initially resists the offer, but Roger
insists, claiming the arrangement was made

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:49.440
in a way that won't arouse suspicion.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.540
"Roger, this is an expensive way to mark your
territory."

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.120
Roger also declares his intention to marry
Megan Draper's mother, Marie, and the last

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:00.700
we see of Roger, he's living a cozy life in
Paris with his new bride.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:05.460
At the beginning of the Mad Men finale, Joan
is living it up in Florida with her wealthy

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:09.380
new beau Richard, enjoying her pseudo-retirement
without a care in the world.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:12.100
Meanwhile, Peggy is struggling to get respect
at McCann.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:14.880
They both seem unsure of what they want to
do with their lives.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.260
But after having dinner with her former colleague
Ken Cosgrove, Joan believes she has an answer...for

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:20.080
both of them.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.240
Ken asks Joan to produce an industrial film
for his company, and Joan decides to hire

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.460
Peggy to write the script.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.700
Peggy is initially reluctant, at least until
she hears how much money is involved.

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.460
"I don't have time to moonlight."

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.940
"It's $1,200 for a ten-page script."

00:05:35.500 --> 00:05:36.000
"Geez.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:36.900
Well.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.000
Anything for a friend..."

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.740
The project goes swimmingly, allowing Joan
to secure more contracts for similar jobs.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:46.900
As she realizes how much she's been missing
work, Joan flirts with the idea of starting

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:49.140
her own industrial film production company.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:50.840
"Harris-Olson.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.540
You need two names to make it sound real."

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:56.960
"Are you serious?"

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:02.380
Peggy has been increasingly unhappy at McCann,
so she begins considering Joan's intriguing

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:02.920
proposal.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.320
But will she go for it?

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.199
As part of his convoluted backstory, Don Draper,
or to call him his real name, Dick Whitman,

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:14.550
befriended Anna Draper, the widow of the real
Don Draper, whose identity Dick stole during

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:15.460
the Korean War.

00:06:15.780 --> 00:06:20.560
Anna remained Don's closest friend and confidant
until her death in season four, and by proxy,

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.280
Don also became close with Anna's adult niece,
Stephanie.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:28.280
Don shows up at their house in L.A., dreadfully
hungover and depressed, and Stephanie isn't

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.300
doing so hot herself.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:33.580
Having just given up her baby and feeling
lost, she's about to head to a hippie retreat

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:35.020
on the California coast.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:38.080
Don, having nothing better to do, decides
to go along with her.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.000
Upon arriving at the retreat, Don is instantly
put off by all the New Age shenanigans.

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:46.639
Even though he's trying to discover who he
is, he's still a conservative, strait-laced

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.000
guy at heart…and he seems to think the whole
retreat is rather silly.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.840
Later, one attendee criticizes Stephanie for
giving up her baby:

00:06:54.180 --> 00:07:00.180
"What I feel when I hear about your baby is
sadness."

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.580
Don attempts to console Stephanie, but it
doesn't go well.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.000
"You're not my family, what's the matter with
you?"

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:12.720
To make matters worse, Stephanie takes Don's
car in the middle of the night, leaving him

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:14.480
alone in every possible way.

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.500
Throughout the Mad Men finale, it seems like
Joan is trying to have it all.

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:22.880
She wants her carefree relationship with Richard,
but she's also excited by the prospect of

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.180
starting her own business.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:28.580
It all comes to a head when Richard calls
her out on her career aspirations, essentially

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.460
asking her to choose between him and her fledgling
business.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:34.360
"Seems like this might be a chance to build
something."

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.140
"Well, it's going to take all your time, all
your energy, and all your attention."

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:43.380
At the end of the scene, Richard wishes her
luck and literally walks out of her life,

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:47.580
leaving Joan single and ready to mingle, with
businesses that need industrial videos, that

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:48.080
is.

00:07:48.260 --> 00:07:52.139
The fact that Joan winds in charge of her
own business is one of the show's most satisfying

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:52.760
developments.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:57.539
After years of being doubted, pushed around,
and sexually harassed, Joan ultimately decides

00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:59.980
to be her own woman and chart her own course.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.740
Upon learning that Stephanie has fled the
hippie retreat, Don has a complete and utter

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:05.340
meltdown.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.300
Alone and abandoned in a strange place, Don
silently reflects on everything he's lost.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.860
He's walked away from his career, feeling
like he no longer belongs there.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.500
He's recently split up with his wife, Megan,
who wants nothing to do with him.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:22.160
His first ex-wife Betty is dying and also
wants nothing to do with him, and he's effectively

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:23.580
had his kids taken away from him.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:27.540
Now, to top it all off, he's been abandoned
and betrayed by someone he considered part

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:28.240
of his family.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:32.740
In this desperate place, Don calls the only
person he thinks might care about him: Peggy

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:33.240
Olson.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:34.960
She initially lashes out at him:

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:36.840
"Where the hell are you?"

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.690
Don's former protégé changes her tune once
Don starts crying, expressing regret about

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.360
all the mistakes he's made in his life.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:46.620
Peggy tries to talk him down, fearing that
he may be suicidal.

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:50.760
Those fears certainly aren't laid to rest
when Don says he's calling to say goodbye.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:54.480
He then abruptly ends the call by saying he
just wanted to hear her voice.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.480
We've seen Don break down before, but never
like this.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:02.480
Peggy is naturally freaked out about her conversation
with Don, and so she gives art director Stan

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:03.800
Rizzo a jingle.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:08.040
He emphasizes that Don is prone to taking
off without telling anyone, assuring Peggy

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.340
that he'll come back eventually.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:10.780
Suddenly, whiplash!

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:13.160
Stan abruptly drops a truth bomb on Peggy:

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.240
"I want to be with you.

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:19.860
I'm in love with you."

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:24.980
The admission takes Peggy completely by surprise,
and at first she doesn't even understand what

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:25.860
Stan is saying.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:30.500
After all, she has a lot on her mind, her
status at McCann, Joan's business proposal,

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.580
her wayward boss.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:34.780
She hasn't really taken any time to think
about her romantic life.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.120
Earlier in the episode, Stan tells Peggy:

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.160
"There's more to life than work."

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:41.780
Now Peggy is starting to see what he's talking
about.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:45.800
And when she takes a moment to consider her
feelings, here's what she comes up with:

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:48.140
"I can't believe this.

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.600
I think I'm in love with you, too."

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:53.940
Soon enough, they kiss and embrace.

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:58.240
It's the finale's biggest bit of fan service,
which doesn't make the moment any less satisfying.

00:09:59.280 --> 00:10:03.480
After speaking with Peggy, Don sits alone,
staring into space until a member of the retreat

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.060
comes along and invites him to sit in on a
group session.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:07.560
Don eventually relents.

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:12.540
During the session, a suburban father, who's
roughly Don's age, has his own tearful breakdown

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:17.140
as he describes how no one appreciates him:
Not his family, not his colleagues, no one.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:19.200
"But I've never been interesting to anybody."

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:23.520
Don sees something of himself in the man and
embraces him, and they hold each other and

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:24.800
sob uncontrollably.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:28.180
The next morning, Don attends a group meditation
outdoors.

00:10:28.530 --> 00:10:32.780
No longer appearing skeptical, cynical, or
broken down, Don meditates along with the

00:10:32.780 --> 00:10:33.600
rest of the group.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.120
Eventually, the faintest hint of a smile creeps
across his face.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:41.580
The episode then cuts to McCann's famous "I'd
Like to Buy the World a Coke" commercial,

00:10:41.580 --> 00:10:45.120
strongly suggesting that Don Draper cooked
up the concept right then and there.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.520
So what's going on here?

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:50.160
Well, one could argue that Don has finally
discovered his true purpose.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:54.000
He pushed away his advertising career to try
and find meaning elsewhere, and he had to

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.040
lose everything to realize he really is truly
an ad man through and through.

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.380
He's the kind of guy who would happily exploit
hippie culture as a means to sell soda made

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.710
by one of the biggest corporations in the
world.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:06.960
Hey, it's nice work if you can get it, right?

